{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-214007",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- Relation $R$ is transitive. Indeed, if $(x,y)\\in R$ and $(y,x)\\in R,$ then $(x,z)\\in R.$ Notice that \n\n$$\n|x|=|y|\\,\\text{and}\\,|y|=|z|\\,⟹\\,|x|=|z|\n$$\n\nfor all $x,y,z\\in R.$\n\n- Relation $S$ is not transitive. Notice that \n\n- $(2,1)\\in S$ since $2∣(1+3),$ and\n\n- $(1,4)\\in S$ since $1∣(4+3),$ but\n\n- $(2,4)\\notin S$ since $2∤(4+3).$\n\n- Relation $T$ is not transitive. Notice that \n\n- $(2,3)\\in T$ since $2∤3,$ and\n\n- $(3,4)\\in T$ since $3∤4,$ but\n\n- $(2,4)\\notin T$ since $2∣4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$R$ only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
